Subject: Question about increment_log_prob()
Message-ID: <8I_ERSED0Rs.4i5ZkYwcp3IJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/8I_ERSED0Rs/m/4i5ZkYwcp3IJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi All,

In the Stan 2.2.0 manual entry on increment_log_prob(), it says:

"Vector arguments are included for convenience and have no speed advantage over adding values in a loop." (p. 266)

Is the lack of speed improvement an inherent limitation to the defining your own likelihoods or could this function be "vectorized" in a way that leads to speed improvements (like the built in distributional forms)? If the latter, is that a feature the Stan team is considering implementing?

I ask because I have a model that uses a mixture likelihood and I am in the process of speeding it up and vectorizing the likelihood seems like a place where my model could improve.

Thanks for your help and the great software.

Best,
Scott

From u368f61d9252655a2 at groups.google.com  Wed Mar 12 20:37:53 2014
From: u368f61d9252655a2 at groups.google.com (Herra Huu)
Date: Wed, 12 Mar 2014 20:37:53 +0000
Subject: Re: Logistic GP
Message-ID: <bLrVjmimJng.LQl5i5qm94EJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <bLrVjmimJng.Iwz7_-iJ6EEJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <bLrVjmimJng.Iwz7_-iJ6EEJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/bLrVjmimJng/m/LQl5i5qm94EJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Simplex transformation doesn't seem to do that much here, in fact I think the sampling become even a bit slower. But using reparameterizations "tricks" for multivariate normal distribution (p. 182-184 in Stan manual) did help a lot (about 4s / 2000 samples compared to 100s with the original model). I'm still learning about Gaussian processes, so I'm not 100% sure if the model is correct though (results seems to be ok). But anyway, here is the code:

data {
  int<lower=1> N;     // number of bins
  vector[N] x;        // bin centers
  int<lower=0> y[N];  // number of observations per bin
}
parameters {

  real<lower=0> ms2;  // magnitude of GP
  real<lower=0> l;    // lengthscale of GP
  real b1;            // mean function parameter
  real<lower=0> b2;   // mean function parameter

  vector[N] alpha;

}
transformed parameters {
  vector[N] mu;

  vector[N] p;
  matrix[N,N] L;
  matrix[N,N] Sigma;

  mu <- b1*x - b2*x .* x;

  for (i in 1:N)
    for (j in 1:N)
    {
      Sigma[i,j] <- ms2*exp(-pow(x[i] - x[j],2)/l) + if_else(i==j, 0.0001, 0.0);

      Sigma[j,i] <- Sigma[i,j];
    }

  L <- cholesky_decompose(Sigma);
  p <- softmax(mu + L * alpha);
}
model {
  alpha ~ normal(0,1);

  // priors for covariance function parameters
  ms2 ~ student_t(4,0,10);
  l ~ student_t(4,0,4);

  // log-prob
  for (n in 1:N)

    increment_log_prob(y[n]*log(p[n]));
}

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Wed Mar 12 20:40:11 2014
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Wed, 12 Mar 2014 20:40:11 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Question about increment_log_prob()
Message-ID: <8I_ERSED0Rs.g4VIqGjuni0J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <8I_ERSED0Rs.4i5ZkYwcp3IJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <8I_ERSED0Rs.4i5ZkYwcp3IJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/8I_ERSED0Rs/m/g4VIqGjuni0J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

There's no way to speed it up because it's already optimized!

Loops are fast in Stan already, because they're just compiled to C++.

In Stan, the speed boost from vectorization comes from reducing
the size of the expression tree involved in automatic differentiation.
The increment_log_prob() function itself uses an accumulator so that
the effect of calling it multiple times stores up the value and then
produces a single node in the expression tree with all the summands
as operands.

More specifically, given an array

  real foo[N];

the Stan code

  for (n in 1:N)
    increment_log_prob(foo[n]);

is translated to C++ as:

  for (int n = 1; n <= N; ++n)
    lp_accumulator.add(foo[n-1]);

where lp_accumulator is an instance of stan::math::accumulator.

In contrast, the Stan code

  increment_log_prob(foo);

is translated to C++ as:

  lp_accumulator.add(foo);

Now the reason they are roughly the same is that the
accumulator class is defined this way:

   template <typename T>
    class accumulator {
    private:
      std::vector<T> buf_;

...
      template <typename S>
      typename boost::enable_if<boost::is_arithmetic<S>, void>::type
      add(S x) {
        buf_.push_back(static_cast<T>(x));
      }

     template <typename S>
      void add(const std::vector<S>& xs) {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < xs.size(); ++i)
          add(xs[i]);
      }
...

So it works out to almost identical C++ code.  There is a bit more indirection
in the vectorized call, but that'll almost certainly be inlined by the compiler.

- Bob

