Hi all!

So, this is something I would like to do. I want to estimate a lower triangular matrix with ones in the main diagonal. I will need the matrix in the transformed parameters block. The idea is that I define a column vector of parameters that contains the off-lower-diagonal elements of the matrix, and then, in the transformed parameters block, I define,

matrix[M,M]             A;
int                     indexA;

// Build matrix A
A          <- diag_matrix(rep_vector(1, M));
indexA     <- 1;

    for(k in 2:M){
        for (m in 1:(k-1)){
            A[k,m]     <- A1[(indexA+m-1)];
        }

        indexA      <- indexA + k - 1;
    }

where A1 is the vector of parameters that I am estimating. However, it is true that the transformed parameters block does not accept integers, and I have defined an auxiliar integer "indexA" to construct the matrix. Obviously, I get an error from STAB... Is there anyway to do this, without constructing the matrix manually cell by cell? (Let's say that M is quite big...)

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Tue Mar 25 18:26:04 2014
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Tue, 25 Mar 2014 18:26:04 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] stan and PK modeling
Message-ID: <7CDHDhrqz-U.GNoqaoAgIqcJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <7CDHDhrqz-U.PiktwfJc3XgJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <7CDHDhrqz-U.PiktwfJc3XgJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/7CDHDhrqz-U/m/GNoqaoAgIqcJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

You can just do this directly in Stan.  First
note that the usual linear regression

  y[i] = alpha * x[i] + beta + epsilon[i];
  epsilon[i] ~ normal(0,sigma);

translates into Stan as follows:

  y[i] ~ normal(alpha * x[i] + beta, sigma);

The epsilon[i] term is marginalized out, but you
can recover it as a generated quantity as
(y[i] - (alpha * x[i] + beta)) if you need the residuals
for something.

You can do the same thing with your system, which
I'm writing fully indexed as:

  y[i] = model[i] + (err1[i] * model[i]) + err2[i];

  err1[i] ~ normal(0,sigma1);
  err2[i] ~ normal(0,sigma2);

This becomes

  y[i] ~ normal(model[i] + (err1[i] * model[i]), sigma2);
  err1[i] ~ normal(0, sigma1);

At least I think that works out to what you want.  With
essentially two error terms and one that varies by item, I'm
curious as to how it'll fit.

What priors were you thinking of using for the sigma?

- Bob

